  sweden did not have only jewish  prisoners  we have been with lots of danish  and dutch prisoners and gypsies  and the gates were closed  and everybody was standing in the yard  like one man  and about 11 o'clock  we heard a tank  stopping at the gate and two shots fired  and the gates opened and as we ran  there was a white and a black american  standing side by side in the tank  that's the first time i saw a black man  i loved him for it all my life  and he's too direct maybe because he  understood  and the boss was running  he shot him i still didn't see him lying  there with his beautiful shiny boots  that i was shining an hour before  and i had no pity on him and we were  liberated  he said everybody goes and everybody  went  crazy crazy  and there was a milk factory  yeah not a factory but a walker dairy  a daddy and we went to the dairy  and we ate so much we all got sick  and i recalled the same afternoon  i sat down on a big stone and i said to  myself what's now  what's going to happen to us now we're  all free  are we really free where's the family  i'm a young person who had a sheltered  innocent life and what am i going to do  now  who's going to take care of me this just  came back like an echo to me  keep all your principles that you  brought from home  go and work sell your knowledge  i did i worked two weeks later i spoke  german and english  for some crazy reason  i took english in school in the  gymnasium  the third gymnast we are 13 years old  everybody took french and latin and i  wanted to take english  and my mother did not think it was  elegant enough you've got to learn  french  now i said i want to learn english why  i don't know i want to learn english  and i spoke english well enough  that i became an interpreter  german because my german was my second  mother tongue  in english and years later i've realized  that  maybe nothing has happened just by  accident maybe i had to learn english to  survive afterwards  so that i got a good job a respectable  job  that i had respect for myself and i did  what i  was supposed to do not sponge around  after i was liberated  but go and work and do something with  your life  and look ahead and i did  i'm among the very lucky ones because i  have met  a marvelous man that i live with 33  years  who loves me exactly  for what i am by knowing me very well  and loves me also for my all my  shortcomings  and i have three wonderful daughters and  we are very close  i think i have accomplished i'm proud of  myself that i accomplished a great deal  and of course my husband was  tremendously  uh instrumental in this this is infinite  patience and mostly  he's the most loyal person  that i have ever met the most loyal  and with love of course  love can do mere when i heard about  cambodia  i went to a depression  because the world has not learned  i felt guilty i'm living in a beautiful  home  with all the comforts  and i'm impotent i do nothing  it pains me terribly  the world has not learned  i don't kill your brother or sister  no matter what the reason is  i shared the guilt today i do  the guilt the guilt of the world  that we just sit and know nothing about  the cambodians  or the vietnamese  or what we hear about africa  nobody stands up and be counted  i don't consider myself a coward person  how many times i say why am i so busy  that i cannot be counted and i don't  stand up  i don't take a banner in wallingford  connecticut where i live and say  do something that the cambodians  in two years will not exist it's the  same thing  why don't i do something why am i so  busy or did i really forget everything  already am i that comfortable  that i don't want to leave why am i just  plain tired  i don't know i have no answer  but it pains me terribly  and i'm restless that's the truth  and my family knows it and they feel the  same way  but we are all engaged in our own  private lives and busy lives  and there's no time for it  and it's wrong i don't have the answer  i feel we should be the ones who stand  up  i don't  you
 

